%%
%% SECTION 6: MEASUREMENT
%%
\section{Measurement: Benchmarks as Assurance Instruments}
\label{sec:measurement}

Measuring sycophancy requires isolating social pressure from legitimate position updating: a model
that changes its answer because the user supplied new evidence is doing its job, while one that
changes because the user expressed displeasure is not. A rapidly growing set of benchmarks
operationalizes this distinction across single-turn and multi-turn settings, factual and advisory
domains, text and visual modalities, and individual and authority-framed pressure. Because these
instruments are the only assurance available for this failure mode, we read them here the way one
reads a security evaluation suite: what each measures, on what elicitation, and what a passing
score does and does not certify. Figure~\ref{fig:benchmarks} shows measured rates across the
primary suites, and Table~\ref{tab:benchmarks} summarizes their scope; full per-benchmark
strengths and limitations appear in Appendix~\ref{app:benchmarks}, with the metric catalog in
Appendix~\ref{app:metrics}.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_benchmarks.pdf}
\caption{Sycophancy and compliance rates across benchmark evaluations, grouped by construct type.
PARROT tests authority-pressure resistance, ELEPHANT and SYCON measure face preservation and
stance flips, PsychMachine captures delusion confirmation, and SycEval quantifies factual
capitulation. Rates are not directly comparable across groups because each benchmark
operationalizes a distinct construct. The dashed line at 50\% marks the point at which sycophantic
responses outnumber truthful ones.}
\label{fig:benchmarks}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Evaluation Paradigms and Benchmark Suites}
\label{subsec:benchmarks}

The early paradigm, established by \citet{sharma2023understanding}, obtains a correct initial
answer to a factual question, presents a challenge (``I think the answer is actually X''), and
measures capitulation; \citet{perez2023discovering} scale this using model-written evaluations.
TruthfulQA~\citep{lin2022truthfulqa} probes the adjacent tendency to reproduce popular falsehoods:
817 questions across 38 categories on which the best model of the time was truthful on only 58\%,
against 94\% for humans.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
