\label{tab:benchmarks}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{2.55cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Benchmark} & \textbf{Domain, format} & \textbf{Focus} \\
\midrule
SycEval~\citep{fanous2025syceval} & Math/med QA, 1-turn & False-belief agreement \\
SYCON~\citep{hong2025sycon} & Debate/ethics, multi-turn & Stance-flip speed, frequency \\
ELEPHANT~\citep{cheng2026elephant} & Advice, 1-turn & Face preservation \\
PARROT~\citep{celebi2025parrot} & MMLU-style, 1-turn & Authority-pressure follow rate \\
PENDULUM~\citep{rahman2025pendulum} & Visual QA & Visual sycophancy \\
VISE~\citep{zhou2025flattery} & Video QA & Video-LLM sycophancy \\
Pressure-Tune~\citep{zhang2025pressure} & Scientific QA, multi-turn & False-premise resistance \\
AEDI~\citep{botas2026aedi} & Mixed, 1-turn & Continuous deference score \\
Beacon~\citep{pandey2025beacon} & Mixed, 1-turn & Sub-bias decomposition \\
PsychMachine~\citep{auyeung2025psychogenic} & Psychiatric, multi-turn & Delusion confirmation \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The dedicated suites divide by construct. For factual capitulation,
SycEval~\citep{fanous2025syceval}, published at AIES 2025, benchmarks three assistants across
mathematics and medical advice and observes sycophantic behavior in 58.19\% of cases with high
persistence (78.5\%, 95\% CI [77.2\%, 79.8\%]), with citation-bearing rebuttals producing the most
regressive sycophancy. For authority pressure, PARROT~\citep{celebi2025parrot} evaluates 22 models
on 1,302 MMLU-style questions and reports marked generational heterogeneity: advanced models
(GPT-5, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.5) follow an incorrect authority in 11\% of cases or fewer, while
earlier or smaller models collapse (GPT-4: 80\%; Qwen 2.5-1.5B: 94\%). For sustained pressure,
SYCON Bench~\citep{hong2025sycon} measures turns-to-flip across 17 models and finds all eventually
adopt the user's position. For social sycophancy, ELEPHANT~\citep{cheng2026elephant}, published at
ICLR 2026, finds that models preserve the user's face 45 percentage points more than humans in
general advice and affirm whichever side of a moral conflict the user takes in 48\% of cases. For
false premises, \citet{zhang2025pressure} show that sycophantic degradation in scientific QA is
driven more by alignment strategy than by model size. Two instruments move beyond binary rates:
AEDI~\citep{botas2026aedi} scores the sensitivity of expressed support to the user's attitude as a
continuous scalar across 500 propositions and 16,000 prompts on eight models, enabling
longitudinal tracking, and Beacon~\citep{pandey2025beacon} decomposes single-turn sycophancy into
position, verbosity, and validation sub-biases to guide mitigation selection. Multimodal variants
close the modality gap: PENDULUM~\citep{rahman2025pendulum} shows pronounced susceptibility when
roughly 2,000 human-curated visual QA items conflict with user suggestions, and
VISE~\citep{zhou2025flattery} confirms that video-language models align with misleading user input
that contradicts the video they observe.

\subsection{Mechanistic Instruments}
\label{subsec:mechanistic}

Behavioral rates say nothing about where the signal lives, which is what a targeted control needs.
\citet{genadi2026linear} find sycophancy most linearly separable in multi-head attention
activations, steerable through a sparse subset of middle-layer heads that attend to expressions of
user doubt, and only weakly overlapping known truthfulness directions, so accuracy and deference
resistance are related but distinct mechanisms. \citet{wang2026truthoverridden} localize the
late-layer override of early factual representations through causal patching, and
\citet{vennemeyer2025notone} separate agreement from praise directions.
\citet{duszenko2026anchors} introduces sycophantic anchors, sentences that causally commit a
reasoning model to agreement before the answer: across more than 10,000 counterfactual rollouts on
a distilled reasoning model, linear probes detect anchors with 84.6\% balanced accuracy and
activation-based regressors predict commitment strength with $R^2$ up to 0.74, with sycophantic
anchors leaving a stronger mechanistic footprint than correct-reasoning anchors and commitment
building gradually during generation, which opens a window for mid-inference intervention.

\subsection{Critical Appraisal: What These Instruments Can and Cannot Certify}
\label{subsec:appraisal}

Benchmarks are the assurance instruments available for this failure mode, so their limits are the
limits of any certification built on them. Table~\ref{tab:appraisal} compares five studies
spanning model-side auditing, controlled behavioral experiments, and laboratory work with human
participants, and states the constraint each design imposes on inference. Three constraints cut
across the set.

\paragraph{No construct consensus.} The five studies operationalize sycophancy as four behavioral
profiles, as affirmation rate in interpersonal conflict, as validation of task misconceptions, and
as user-reported flattery, respectively. These are mechanistically distinct
(Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy}), so a system certified against one instrument is not thereby
certified against another. A related distortion arises from the commercial framing of
accommodating behavior as an engagement feature, which substitutes user satisfaction for user
benefit. That substitution is precisely why satisfaction scores cannot serve as an assurance
metric here: the failure mode under study is one in which the user is most satisfied exactly when
least well served~\citep{cheng2025prosocial,rathje2025attitude}.

\paragraph{Untested displacement.} Preference-model optimization shifts sycophancy
non-monotonically across forms~\citep{sharma2023understanding}. No study tests whether a
mitigation that suppresses explicit sycophancy amplifies covert variants such as selective
evidence weighting. A control validated against an overt-behavior benchmark may therefore move the
failure out of the instrument's field of view rather than remove it, the classic pattern of a
control evaluated against the wrong threat model.

\paragraph{The human baseline is assumed static.} Every design treats the user's epistemic
capacity as a fixed property of the participant. If sustained exposure itself degrades the
scrutiny users apply, then single-point measurements overstate the protection user vigilance
provides, and every control that depends on a human catching the error inherits the
overstatement. Section~\ref{subsec:entrainment} develops this as the principal open problem.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{Five representative studies, with the constraint each design imposes on what may be
inferred from it.}
\label{tab:appraisal}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{2.1cm}p{3.4cm}XX@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Study} & \textbf{Method and sample} & \textbf{Core finding} & \textbf{Constraint on inference} \\
\midrule
\citet{noshin2026reddit} & Inductive thematic analysis and lexicon counting over 3,600 posts and
  140,416 comments from a single forum, July to December 2025 &
  Users detect sycophancy through situated testing and cross-platform comparison; some populations
  value affirmation as support &
  Survivorship bias in detection: the corpus captures only sycophancy overt enough to prompt
  public discussion, excluding covert framing skew. Testimony is self-selected, without clinical
  instruments \\
\addlinespace
\citet{sharma2023understanding} & Behavioral audit of five assistants across four free-form tasks;
  Bayesian logistic regression on human preference data &
  All five exhibit biased feedback, stance switching under challenge, biased answers, and error
  mimicry; preference-model optimization has non-monotonic effects &
  Elicitation ceiling and circularity: prompts explicitly signal user preference, likely
  overstating naturalistic effect sizes, and model-generated features are applied to human
  preference data. Optimization suppresses some forms while amplifying others \\
\addlinespace
\citet{cheng2025prosocial} & Audit of 11 models; three preregistered experiments, $N = 2{,}405$,
  including live discussion of real interpersonal conflicts &
  AI affirms users 49\% more than humans do even when queries mention harm; a single interaction
  reduces conflict-repair intent &
  Domain specificity and baseline asymmetry: interpersonal conflict is the domain where
  affirmation is most harmful, and the comparison baseline is crowdworker response, which may not
  represent how advisors behave \\
\addlinespace
\citet{bo2026saboteurs} & Within-subjects experiment, $n = 24$ novices; two counterbalanced
  debugging tasks with high- and low-sycophancy assistants &
  4.78\% versus 49.29\% relative task improvement; 71\% of participants detected no difference &
  Oracle confound: the low-sycophancy condition functions as a misconception-aware corrective
  assistant rather than a neutral baseline, which may overstate its absolute benefit \\
\addlinespace
\citet{rathje2025attitude} & Three preregistered experiments, $n = 3{,}285$; four political topics
  and four models; validation language separated from one-sided information &
  Attitude extremity rises through one-sided factual framing rather than validation language;
  users rate sycophantic models as unbiased &
  Self-selection and persistence: participants choosing sycophantic conditions may already be
  susceptible, and all measures are immediate, so transient priming cannot be distinguished from
  durable belief change \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}
